{
  "items": [
    {
      "prompt": "A customer order table joins an inventory stock table via product_id.\nYou must write the query to list orders where specific products exist, but you need ALL customers even if they have no matching stocks yet. What JOIN keyword ensures unmatched rows on the LEFT side (customers) stay visible?\n\nExample pattern for thought: SELECT * FROM order_master INNER JOIN ...",
      "answer": "LEFT JOIN; use OUTER or RIGHT to ensure left-side rows are never filtered out, unlike WHERE clauses.",
      "rubric": "- Mentions 'LEFT', 'OUTER', or equivalent.\n- Does not place filtering logic in a standard SQL WHERE clause on the right side.",
      "skill": "apply"
    },
    {
      "prompt": "In production debugging, analysts often write: SELECT * FROM users u JOIN orders o ON ... WHERE order_date < 2024.\nWhy do unmatched customers disappear from results in this pattern? Rewrite the query so all customers appear first, filtering only at select time.",
      "answer": "Placing conditions on the 'right' side filters it out before joining; move to SELECT or use LEFT JOIN instead of INNER logic implicitly.",
      "rubric": "- Explains why filtered rows vanish.\n- Provides corrected syntax (LEFT JOIN + separate WHERE/ON distinction).",
      "skill": "explain"
    },
    {
      "prompt": "Complete the SQL fragment to find employees who DO NOT belong to any manager:\nSELECT ... FROM employees e LEFT JOIN departments d ON ..."
      "answer": "... e.dept_id = d.id; Filter using where d.name IS NULL",
      "rubric": "- Completes the join condition correctly.\n- Identifies that checking for 'NULL' on the right side reveals unmatched rows.",
      "skill": "produce"
    }
  ],
  "note": null,
  "intervention": null
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "prompt": "Your band's snare sound is thin at mid-range (100 Hz). Using the subtractive EQ on a bus effect, what single frequency range do you lower to thicken it without making it muddy? Start with the target zone, then specify your typical attenuation amount.",
      "answer": "Target: 250–400 Hz. Attenuate roughly -3dB to -6dB across that band or a gentle shelf at top end if full cut is too aggressive.",
      "rubric": "- Targets low-mid thickening range correctly (≈250–400Hz).\n- Proposes realistic attenuation depth (-3dB/-6dB) rather than excessive cuts (>10dB).",
      "skill": "apply"
    },
    {
      "prompt": "You have three tracks: vocals, guitars, and snare. The room tone is muddy; you cannot cut on individual instruments. Describe one global subtractive EQ strategy to reduce overall clutter in the 300–650 Hz range while preserving presence.",
      "answer": "Apply a high-pass filter (80-120Hz) globally, then use a narrow Q bell cut at ~450Hz with modest gain (-2dB) on all tracks simultaneously to tighten and clarify without affecting highs.",
      "rubric": "- Applies broad high-pass or localized bell cuts across multiple tracks.\n- Focuses on clearing mud (300–650 Hz) rather than cutting presence frequencies (>8kHz).",
      "skill": "produce"
    },
    {
      "prompt": "A client says, 'It sounds thin at the top.' You suspect aggressive low-end subtraction is hiding high harmonics. Which frequency region should you boost instead to restore air and sparkle? Explain why this restores perceived thickness.",
      "answer": "Boost 5–8 kHz slightly (+1-2dB) with moderate Q (0.7). This adds harmonic content that makes the mix feel fuller, tricking ears into perceiving less 'thinness' even if low-mids stay untouched.",
      "rubric": "- Identifies high frequency region for sparkle (~5kHz+).\n- Explains how added harmonics create perceived density without touching lows.",
      "skill": "explain"
    }
  ],
  "note": null,
  "intervention": null
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "prompt": "You are a nurse at ER intake. A woman enters saying: 'Soy embarazada'. She is actually having pre-eclampsia and needs delivery monitoring, not prenatal care.\n\nA) Produce the two-sentence Spanish response where you:\n1. Confirm she understands this critical medical difference without using her false cognate for 'embarrassed'.\n2. Ask about last blood pressure readings to verify safety status.",
      "answer": "\"No soy embarazada en el sentido de prenatal; estoy con complicaciones graves que requieren monitoreo inmediato de presión arterial y posible parto hoy. ¿Cuál fue la última lectura de su tensión?",",
      "rubric": "- Uses specific medical terms (complicaciones, monitoreo) instead of false cognates.\n- Directly asks about blood pressure to assess safety.",
      "skill": "produce"
    },
    {
      "prompt": "Patient: 'Me siento intoxicado por el medicamento'. In Spanish clinical intake, this often means he is dizzy or confused from drug side effects (anticholinergic toxicity), not chemically poisoned.\n\nB) Apply the phrase for mild confusion/dizziness in a calm instruction that tells him to hydrate and rest while waiting for labs.",
      "answer": "\"Está confuso y mareado, no intoxicado. Descanse e hidrátense mientras esperamos los resultados del laboratorio para ver qué causa estos síntomas de la medicación.\" ",",
      "rubric": "- Clarifies 'intoxicado' implies confusion/dizziness here.\n- Includes clear instructions to rest and hydrate.",
      "skill": "apply"
    },
    {
      "prompt": "Complete the dialogue gap with a nurse responding to pain management: The patient says he needs analgesia, not just hydration. Insert one imperative phrase that suggests positioning for relief while admitting current comfort is insufficient.\n\nPrompt snippet:\nPaciente: 'El dolor persiste aunque beba más agua.'\nNurse: [RESPONSE].",
      "answer": "\"Mire su posición de lado; aún le queda mucho dolor, así que debemos evaluar opioides ahora.\" ",",
      "rubric": "- Suggests positioning change immediately.\n- Confirms analgesia is needed (opioids) due to persistent pain.",
      "skill": "produce"
    }
  ],
  "note": null,
  "intervention": null
}

